// Practice quiz: Preventing cross-site scripting
// First draft: unreviewed. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: html-css-js/dom-tooling-and-async/preventing-cross-site-scripting

::o1 why handlers run::A string with an image tag and an onerror attribute is assigned to innerHTML. Why can it run code? {
=The string is parsed as HTML, and the handler attribute is active once the element exists #Correct. Data has become markup with a live handler.
~Images always execute JavaScript #Not quite. Ordinary images do not.
~innerHTML calls eval on every string #Not quite. It parses HTML rather than evaluating JavaScript.
~The browser treats failed images as scripts #Not quite. The failed load only fires the error handler.
}

::o1 script via innerHTML::A script element inserted through innerHTML does not run. What can you conclude? {
=Nothing about safety, because event handler attributes can still run #Correct. The sink remains risky.
~innerHTML is safe for untrusted input #Not quite. Handlers and other vectors still work.
~Browsers remove all markup from innerHTML #Not quite. Markup is parsed and kept.
~Only inline scripts are blocked by default #Not quite. That does not make the sink safe either.
}

::o2 choosing the API::You must show a username from an API inside a span. Which line fits? {
=span.textContent \= username #Correct. The value stays plain text whatever it contains.
~span.innerHTML \= username #Not quite. That parses the value as HTML.
~span.outerHTML \= username #Not quite. That also parses HTML and replaces the element.
~document.write(username) #Not quite. That writes raw HTML into the document.
}

::o3 hidden sink::A profile link does a.href \= user.website. What should you add? {
=A check that the URL scheme is http or https #Correct. A javascript scheme would otherwise run code when followed.
~Nothing, because href is always text #Not quite. URL attributes can carry active schemes.
~Setting a.textContent instead of href #Not quite. That no longer makes a link.
~A CSS class on the link #Not quite. Styling has no effect on the scheme.
}

::o3 rich text::Users must be able to write bold and italic comments. What is the appropriate approach? {
=Sanitize with a maintained HTML sanitizer before inserting, plus a Content Security Policy #Correct. Rich text needs parsing, so use a vetted allowlist sanitizer.
~Write your own regular expression to strip script tags #Not quite. Handwritten filters miss many vectors.
~Use textContent and show the markup literally #Not quite. That is safe, but it does not meet the rich text need.
~Trust the input because users are logged in #Not quite. A logged-in user can still attack others.
}
